T1  - A retrospective study of growth hormone use in adolescents with cystic fibrosis.
DO  - https://dx.doi.org/10.1111/j.1365-2265.2005.02259.x
A1  - Hardin, Dana S
A1  - Ferkol, Tom
A1  - Ahn, Chul
A1  - Dreimane, Daina
A1  - Dyson, Maynard
A1  - Morse, Martha
A1  - Prestidge, Claude
A1  - Rice, Julie
A1  - Seilheimer, Dan K
Y1  - 2005//
N2  - <ovid:b>OBJECTIVES</ovid:b>: Studies of growth hormone (GH) effectiveness in prepubertal children with cystic fibrosis (CF) have been published previously. We present a retrospective study of GH treatment in adolescents with CF., <ovid:b>STUDY DESIGN</ovid:b>: We performed a retrospective evaluation of data from 25 pubertal adolescents (ages 13 years 4 months to 16 years 11 months, Tanner stage III or IV). Thirteen (4 F) were followed for 1 year, then received 1 year of treatment with GH (GHTX). We compared the results with a 'control' group of 12 (4 F) whose growth was followed for 1 year (NonTX) but who were not treated with GH at the time of this review. Anthropometric data included: height, weight, lean tissue mass (LTM) and bone mineral content (BMC). Pulmonary function, number of hospitalizations, glycosylated haemoglobin (HbA1c), random blood glucose levels, IGF-I, oestradiol and testosterone levels are also reported., <ovid:b>RESULTS</ovid:b>: There was no difference between the groups at baseline. After 1 year, GHTX had significantly greater height and weight velocity, height and weight Z-scores, body mass index (BMI), LTM and BMC. Absolute pulmonary function was better and hospitalizations were fewer in the GHTX. No subject developed glucose intolerance or had any other side-effects., <ovid:b>CONCLUSION</ovid:b>: These results suggest that GH use in pubertal adolescents with CF safely improves height, body weight, bone mineralization and clinical status.
