T1  - Plasma renin activity and albumin excretion in teenage type I diabetic subjects. A prospective study.
DO  - https://dx.doi.org/10.1161/01.hyp.13.6.781
A1  - Paulsen, E P
A1  - Seip, R L
A1  - Ayers, C R
A1  - Croft, B Y
A1  - Kaiser, D L
Y1  - 1989//
N2  - Plasma renin activity (PRA) may be high among teenage and young adult insulin-dependent diabetic subjects. Supine PRA and stimulated PRA were therefore measured in 50 female and 50 male diabetic subjects, 13-20 years old, diagnosed before the age of 16. Fifty percent have been restudied after 4.6 +/- 0.2 (mean +/- SEM) years. Initially, 43% had high PRA (supine 4.0 +/- 0.37, stimulated 12.02 +/- 0.8 ng/ml/hr angiotensin I), 45% had normal activity (supine 2.89 +/- 0.26, stimulated 6.47 +/- 0.34 ng/ml/hr/angiotensin I), and 12% had low activity (supine 1.57 +/- 0.05, stimulated 3.09 +/- 0.08 ng/ml/hr/angiotensin I). Levels were directly associated with prepubertal duration of diabetes and were inversely associated with duration of diabetes after onset of puberty but not with total duration or patient age. Within 4.6 +/- 0.2 years the percentage of subjects with high PRA fell to 13%, and the percentage of those with low PRA rose to 35%. Initially 51% of the cohort had normal albumin excretion rates (AER) at rest and during exercise equal to or less than 10 micrograms/min/m2; 32% had elevated rates only during exercise of 39 +/- 5 micrograms/min/m2; 13% had elevated rates at rest of 41 +/- 8 micrograms/min/m2 and during exercise of 116 +/- 21 micrograms/min/m2; and 4% had clinical proteinuria at rest and during each exercise period equal to or greater than 150 micrograms/min/m2. After 5 years, 58% continued to have normal AER, or their AER improved.(ABSTRACT TRUNCATED AT 250 WORDS)
